    // ----- Generators -----
    // There are no coroutines in the value model - a thunk cannot be suspended half way
    // - so a generator is driven by REPLAY: every next() re-runs the body from the top,
    // hands the yields that already happened the values they were sent, and stops at the
    // next one by throwing a signal the driver catches. A pure generator behaves exactly
    // right; one with side effects repeats them, which is the honest price of not having
    // a stack to freeze. genRuns is the stack of active replays, innermost last, so a
    // generator driven from inside another generator still finds its own run.
    //
    // .return(v) is present and answers {value: v, done: true}, but it only sets the
    // done flag: the compiler half's gen_close (runtime.c) runs the body's finally
    // clauses at that point, where replay has already run them once per next(). That
    // ordering is the same replay price as above, and it is why a for-of that BREAKS
    // closes its iterator in BOTH halves - a second loop over the generator answers
    // nothing in both - while only the compiler half prints the finally at the break.
    var genRuns = []

